1. [THEOREM] Sum of Divisors Equals Sum of Proper Divisors Plus Self
Module: Mathlib.NumberTheory.Divisors
Name: Nat.sum_divisors_eq_sum_properDivisors_add_self
Description: For any natural number $n$, the sum of all divisors of $n$ is equal to the sum of the proper divisors of $n$ plus $n$ itself, i.e.,
\[ \sum_{d \mid n} d = \left( \sum_{\substack{d \mid n \\ d \neq n}} d \right) + n. \]
Signature:  : ∑ i ∈ divisors n, i = (∑ i ∈ properDivisors n, i) + n

2. [THEOREM] Sum of Proper Divisors Divides Implies Sum is 1 or n
Module: Mathlib.NumberTheory.Divisors
Name: Nat.sum_properDivisors_dvd
Description: For a natural number $n$, if the sum of its proper divisors divides $n$, then this sum is either equal to $1$ or equal to $n$. In other words, if $\sum_{d \in \text{properDivisors}(n)} d$ divides $n$, then $\sum_{d \in \text{properDivisors}(n)} d = 1$ or $\sum_{d \in \text{properDivisors}(n)} d = n$.
Signature:  (h : (∑ x ∈ n.properDivisors, x) ∣ n) : ∑ x ∈ n.properDivisors, x = 1 ∨ ∑ x ∈ n.properDivisors, x = n

3. [THEOREM] Sum of Proper Divisors Equals One if and only if Prime
Module: Mathlib.NumberTheory.Divisors
Name: Nat.sum_properDivisors_eq_one_iff_prime
Description: For a natural number $n$, the sum of its proper divisors equals $1$ if and only if $n$ is a prime number. In other words, $\sum_{x \in \text{properDivisors}(n)} x = 1 \leftrightarrow \text{Prime}(n)$.
Signature:  : ∑ x ∈ n.properDivisors, x = 1 ↔ n.Prime

4. [THEOREM] Self-Dual Property of Divisors under Division: $\{n/x \mid x \in \text{divisors}(n)\} = \text{divisors}(n)$
Module: Mathlib.NumberTheory.Divisors
Name: Nat.image_div_divisors_eq_divisors
Description: For any natural number $n$, the image of the function $x \mapsto n/x$ applied to the set of divisors of $n$ is equal to the set of divisors of $n$ itself. In symbols:
$$\left\{ \frac{n}{x} \mid x \in \text{divisors}(n) \right\} = \text{divisors}(n).$$
Signature:  (n : ℕ) : image (fun x : ℕ => n / x) n.divisors = n.divisors

5. [THEOREM] Sum of Divisors Formula via Prime Factorization: $\sum_{d \mid n} d = \prod_{p \mid n} \sum_{k=0}^{v_p(n)} p^k$
Module: Mathlib.NumberTheory.ArithmeticFunction
Name: Nat.sum_divisors
Description: For any nonzero natural number $n$, the sum of its divisors equals the product over its prime factors $p$ of the sum of powers $p^k$ for $k$ from $0$ to the exponent of $p$ in the factorization of $n$. That is,
\[
\sum_{d \mid n} d = \prod_{p \mid n} \sum_{k=0}^{v_p(n)} p^k,
\]
where $v_p(n)$ denotes the exponent of the prime $p$ in the factorization of $n$.
Signature:  {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 0) : ∑ d ∈ n.divisors, d = ∏ p ∈ n.primeFactors, ∑ k ∈ .range (n.factorization p + 1), p ^ k
