The values increase strictly with \(p\).  
Only \(p=11\) gives a sum below \(2019\) (namely \(1464\)), and the next prime already exceeds the interval.  
Consequently **no** integer of the form (1) lies in \([2010,2019]\).

---

#### 3.2  The product‑of‑two‑primes case (2)

We need primes \(p<q\) such that  

\[
(p+1)(q+1)\in[2010,2019].
\tag{3}
\]

Because the interval is tiny, we can inspect each \(n\) in it and factor it as a product of two integers \(a\) and \(b\) with \(a\le b\).  
Set \(p=a-1,\;q=b-1\); we must check whether both \(p\) and \(q\) are prime.

| \(n\) | factorisation \(a\cdot b\) | \(p=a-1\) | \(q=b-1\) | primality of \(p,q\) |
|------|----------------------------|----------|----------|----------------------|
|2010| \(2\cdot1005,\;3\cdot670,\;5\cdot402,\;6\cdot335,\;10\cdot201,\;15\cdot134,\;30\cdot67\) | none give both primes |
|2011| prime → impossible |
|2012| \(4\cdot503\) gives \(p=3\) (prime) and \(q=502\) (not prime) … other pairs fail |
|2013| \(3\cdot671\) gives \(p=2\) (prime) and \(q=670\) (not prime) … |
|2014| no pair yields two primes |
|2015| \(5\cdot403,\;13\cdot155,\;31\cdot65\) … none work |
|2016| **two successful pairs**:  

  \(4\cdot504\) gives \(p=3\) (prime), \(q=503\) (prime);  

  \(8\cdot252\) gives \(p=7\) (prime), \(q=251\) (prime). |
|2017| prime |
|2018| \(2\cdot1009\) gives \(p=1\) (not prime); other pairs fail |
|2019| \(3\cdot673\) gives \(p=2\) (prime), \(q=672\) (not prime) … |

Only for \(n=2016\) does (3) hold with *both* \(p\) and \(q\) prime.  
Hence the **unique** integer in the interval that can be written as \((p+1)(q+1)\) with distinct primes \(p,q\) is \(n=2016\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Determination of the set \(S\)

From the analysis above we have proved:

*If \(n\in S\) then \(n=2016\).*  

Conversely, take the primes \(p=3\) and \(q=503\).  
Define \(m:=p\cdot q = 1509\).  
The divisor set of \(m\) is \(\{1,3,503,1509\}\); therefore  

\[
(\operatorname{divisors} m).card =4,\qquad
\sum_{d\in\operatorname{divisors} m} d = (p+1)(q+1)=4\cdot504 =2016 .
\]

Moreover \(2010\le2016\le2019\).  
Thus the right‑hand side of \(h₀\) holds for \(n=2016\), and by \(h₀\) we obtain \(2016\in S\).

Consequently  

\[
S = \{\,2016\,\}.
\tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Summation over \(S\)

Because \(S\) is the singleton \(\{2016\}\),

\[
\sum_{k\in S} k = 2016 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Conclusion  

Using the characterisation \(h₀\) together with the elementary classification of numbers having exactly four divisors, we have shown that the only element of \(S\) is \(2016\); therefore the required sum equals \(2016\).

∎

